{-
   The stops - Design-Test.md §5.3's `@test(aborts)`.

   **This is the corpus a library could not have until now.** A failed check and a subscript past the
   end both reach `checkFailed`, which stops the process - so a fixture that asserted one would have
   taken the driver down with it, and every line of that behaviour was something the library
   described and nothing ran. The verdict is inverted: the case passes when the child stops with the
   abort status and fails when it returns normally, which is why each of these is one deliberate stop
   and nothing else.

   `aborts` needs a *process*, so these cases are skipped where the runner has no path to
   re-execute - see `Test.harnessAvailable`, and the note there about what JITs a fixture. Under
   `YanaElfTest`, where the kernel starts the program, they run.
-}

import Test

-- The bare stop, which is where every check below lands. Stated first, so that a failure in one of
-- the others is a failure of *that* check rather than of the mechanism.
@test(aborts) fn statedInvariant() -> {}:
    assert(1 + 1 == 3, "arithmetic")

{-
   A subscript past the end, which is a check the compiler inserts rather than one a program wrote.

   The index is one the optimizer cannot fold, because a constant one is a check it removes - and a
   fixture asserting the removed form would pass by not aborting, for the wrong reason.
-}
@test(aborts) fn subscriptPastTheEnd() -> {}:
    let &xs = [1, 2, 3]
    let &at = 0 :: Size

    while at < (10 :: Size):
        push(xs, xs[at] + 1)
        at = at + 2

-- Emptying a container and then reading its first element: the same check, reached through a length
-- the program itself changed.
@test(aborts) fn emptiedThenRead() -> {}:
    let &xs = [7]
    remove(xs, 0)
    print("{xs[0 :: Size]}\n")

-- What must *not* pass - a case declared `aborts` that returns - is the runner's rule rather than
-- something this file can state, and `test/resolve/Reject.Test.yana` is where the attribute's own
-- shape is checked.
